WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.095
PRAKASH: Namaste, Elias.

00:00:01.095 --> 00:00:02.786
ELIAS: Oh, hi, there, Prakash.

00:00:02.786 --> 00:00:05.990
PRAKASH: [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

00:00:05.990 --> 00:00:06.816
ELIAS: What?

00:00:06.816 --> 00:00:07.830
PRAKASH: I'm sorry.

00:00:07.830 --> 00:00:09.784
What I mean is, you seem happy.

00:00:09.784 --> 00:00:10.950
How's the day going for you?

00:00:10.950 --> 00:00:12.180
ELIAS: I'm doing fine.

00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:14.490
But, Prakash, how many
languages do you speak?

00:00:14.490 --> 00:00:17.390
PRAKASH: Well, I speak Hindi,
Konkani, my mother tongue,

00:00:17.390 --> 00:00:19.125
Kannada, and a couple more.

00:00:19.125 --> 00:00:21.120
ELIAS: Woah, that's impressive.

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:22.680
You know, our
customers have users

00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:25.170
that speak many
languages, and Google has

00:00:25.170 --> 00:00:26.790
tools to help translate texts.

00:00:26.790 --> 00:00:30.570
But how do we help customers
with industry-specific jargon

00:00:30.570 --> 00:00:32.751
or technical material that
needs to be translated

00:00:32.751 --> 00:00:33.750
to a different language?

00:00:33.750 --> 00:00:37.110
PRAKASH: You mean like
"server" in a restaurant

00:00:37.110 --> 00:00:41.445
should be translated differently
from "server" in a data center?

00:00:41.445 --> 00:00:42.880
ELIAS: Exactly.

00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:44.700
PRAKASH: Well,
Elias, Google Cloud

00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:47.110
has created just
the thing for this.

00:00:47.110 --> 00:00:49.620
It's called AutoML Translate.

00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:51.720
It is a way for our
customers to bring

00:00:51.720 --> 00:00:54.600
their business-specific
translation examples

00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:57.660
and easily train a custom
machine-learning model

00:00:57.660 --> 00:00:59.410
to understand their domain.

00:00:59.410 --> 00:01:00.805
Let me show you how it's done.

00:01:00.805 --> 00:01:02.940
ELIAS: Let's do it.

00:01:02.940 --> 00:01:05.820
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:11.690
PRAKASH: This is the AutoML
Translation user interface,

00:01:11.690 --> 00:01:13.920
and you're looking at some
of the custom models that

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:15.880
are already trained.

00:01:15.880 --> 00:01:19.850
Now let's look at some datasets
that are already in place.

00:01:19.850 --> 00:01:22.810
These are used to train
up the custom models.

00:01:22.810 --> 00:01:25.090
Let's create a new dataset.

00:01:25.090 --> 00:01:26.310
First we'll give it a name.

00:01:29.080 --> 00:01:33.000
Then we'll select the source
and target languages--

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:36.980
English and Japanese
in this case.

00:01:36.980 --> 00:01:40.420
The translation examples can
be supplied in two formats--

00:01:40.420 --> 00:01:43.150
TSV and TMX files.

00:01:43.150 --> 00:01:45.610
And you can manually
break this up

00:01:45.610 --> 00:01:50.040
into training, testing,
and validation datasets,

00:01:50.040 --> 00:01:53.580
or you can let the system
do it automatically.

00:01:53.580 --> 00:01:56.010
You can upload the
files from your drive

00:01:56.010 --> 00:01:58.500
or drop them on
Google Cloud Storage.

00:01:58.500 --> 00:02:03.630
For this demo, I'll
pick a file on my drive.

00:02:03.630 --> 00:02:07.510
Clicking on Create Dataset
starts the import process,

00:02:07.510 --> 00:02:10.002
and this can take a
little while depending

00:02:10.002 --> 00:02:11.210
upon the size of the dataset.

00:02:14.490 --> 00:02:19.020
For this demo, I already have
a dataset imported, so let's

00:02:19.020 --> 00:02:22.200
click through on it.

00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:26.200
And on the first screen, you'll
see some sample sentences

00:02:26.200 --> 00:02:28.060
drawn from this dataset.

00:02:28.060 --> 00:02:31.360
On the Training tab, there's
additional information

00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:33.490
about this dataset.

00:02:33.490 --> 00:02:38.190
And you can launch the training
by clicking on Start Training.

00:02:38.190 --> 00:02:40.800
You can pick the base
model from Google

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:42.960
and train up your
custom model, or you

00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:46.500
can continue training a
previously created custom model

00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:48.640
as well.

00:02:48.640 --> 00:02:51.600
And under the covers, the
system automatically deploys

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:56.040
the necessary resources
and handles the training.

00:02:56.040 --> 00:03:00.040
Now, this can take a few hours,
so I have pre-trained a model

00:03:00.040 --> 00:03:02.560
already for use in this demo.

00:03:02.560 --> 00:03:05.970
So let's go access that model.

00:03:05.970 --> 00:03:08.780
Again, when you go
to the Train tab,

00:03:08.780 --> 00:03:12.620
you will see some metrics
for this custom model.

00:03:12.620 --> 00:03:15.380
The system automatically
generates a blue score

00:03:15.380 --> 00:03:17.120
for the custom model.

00:03:17.120 --> 00:03:20.390
And if the blue score is
higher than the base model,

00:03:20.390 --> 00:03:23.480
it means that translations
done using this model

00:03:23.480 --> 00:03:26.390
will be closer to your
reference dataset.

00:03:26.390 --> 00:03:30.140
Now, this dataset has
relatively fewer sentence pairs,

00:03:30.140 --> 00:03:32.720
so these numbers
are not typical.

00:03:32.720 --> 00:03:35.210
In your case, with
larger datasets,

00:03:35.210 --> 00:03:38.300
you will see much
better blue scores.

00:03:38.300 --> 00:03:41.310
You can, of course,
continue to train this model

00:03:41.310 --> 00:03:45.980
and either create a new version
or continue to train this up

00:03:45.980 --> 00:03:48.590
until you get the quality
that you're looking for.

00:03:51.780 --> 00:03:54.980
Now, once you have the
quality that you need,

00:03:54.980 --> 00:03:57.560
you can go to the Predict area.

00:03:57.560 --> 00:04:00.020
And in this case, the
system will show you

00:04:00.020 --> 00:04:05.570
samples for invoking the custom
model via REST API or Python

00:04:05.570 --> 00:04:07.980
code snippets.

00:04:07.980 --> 00:04:10.020
And on this screen,
you can quickly

00:04:10.020 --> 00:04:13.980
run some translation examples
and compare the results

00:04:13.980 --> 00:04:16.860
from your custom model with
the base model from Google.

00:04:16.860 --> 00:04:19.329
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:04:19.329 --> 00:04:23.760
So this is how Google Cloud is
making machine learning easily

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:26.940
accessible to everyone by
addressing the two major

00:04:26.940 --> 00:04:30.110
challenges that slow down
machine-learning projects--

00:04:30.110 --> 00:04:33.240
one, a need for deep
data science skills,

00:04:33.240 --> 00:04:35.430
and two, a need to
manage and deploy

00:04:35.430 --> 00:04:37.129
your own compute resources.

00:04:37.129 --> 00:04:38.670
ELIAS: This is really
great, Prakash.

00:04:38.670 --> 00:04:39.266
Thank you.

00:04:39.266 --> 00:04:41.640
If you really like this video,
please go ahead and share,

00:04:41.640 --> 00:04:43.230
comment, and subscribe.

00:04:43.230 --> 00:04:45.270
And also, if you're working
on any cool projects

00:04:45.270 --> 00:04:46.980
and would like to get
in contact with us,

00:04:46.980 --> 00:04:49.140
please click on the
link in the description.

00:04:49.140 --> 00:04:51.810
Thanks for watching.

00:04:51.810 --> 00:04:55.160
[MUSIC PLAYING]

